# watchers — the harness's own rows for what should be watching.
#
#   name|kind|target[|health]
#
# NOT THE ONLY MANIFEST. Every `*.watchers` file in SPIRA_WATCHERS_OVERLAY (default
# ~/.config/spira/watchers.d/) is read the same way and merged into the same rows, so an
# operator's or the Concierge's own watcher joins this one without a change here — see
# watchd for why a name must still be unique across both.
#
# ONE ROW IS ONE WATCHER, and one `daemon` row is one systemd unit: `install.sh` renders
# `spira-watch@.service` and enables `spira-watch@<name>.service` for every daemon row here,
# and disables any instance whose row has gone. Adding a watcher is a row plus an install
# run — never a new unit file, because a unit file is a path baked onto one box.
#
# KINDS
#
#   daemon   we own the process. The target is a program that loops forever; systemd runs
#            it through `watchd exec <name>` and appends its stdout to the watcher's log.
#            NOTHING ELSE MAY BE RUNNING IT. A target that notices a second copy and exits
#            CLEANLY is restarted anyway — `Restart=always` restarts on exit 0 too — so a
#            copy somebody started by hand turns its unit into a loop that spawns and dies on
#            every RestartSec. Retire the hand-started one when you add the row.
#   log      something else already writes that log — a cron job, another harness — so the
#            target is the log file itself and there is no unit. Running it here as well
#            would double it up.
#
# and one you never write, only read back: `off`, what a `?` row becomes when the key it
# names is unset. Its target is the empty placeholder, it gets no unit, and `status` renders
# it as not installed.
#
# THE CONTRACT A READER LATCHES ONTO is two files per watcher and nothing else:
#
#   <SPIRA_RUN>/watchd/<name>.log      newline-delimited events, append-only
#   <SPIRA_RUN>/watchd/<name>.cursor   an integer: lines already delivered
#
# so `tail -n +$((cursor+1)) -F <log>` is a conforming client. That is what keeps this
# agent-agnostic: a session attaches to a file, and owns no process.
#
# TARGETS ARE ABSOLUTE, AND PATHS COME FROM CONFIGURATION. Write `@SPIRA_COCKPIT@/x.sh`, not
# a path off this box: the placeholder is filled from spira.conf, so an operator who moves
# the cockpit does not have to remember this file. A placeholder that is unknown, or that
# resolves to empty because the key is an optional one nobody set, REFUSES the manifest —
# an empty expansion would silently produce `/x.sh` and run whatever is there. A relative
# target is refused for the same reason: systemd starts a unit with no working directory
# worth the name, so a relative path resolves against `/`.
#
# A LEADING `?` ON THE NAME IS THE ONE EXEMPTION, and only from the empty-key half of that.
# It marks a row as watching something not every installation has, so an unset key renders it
# as kind `off`: `status` prints a line saying the watcher exists and is not installed, and
# nothing tries to start it. Without the marker, one row for a program the operator has not
# got would take every OTHER watcher on the box down with it, because a manifest that refuses
# installs none of itself. An unknown placeholder, a bad kind or a relative target still refuse
# the file even in a marked row — those are faults whatever the configuration says.
#
# A TARGET IS NOT SHELL. It is a program and its arguments, split on whitespace. Nothing is
# expanded, globbed or substituted — a row that needs shell should point at a script that is
# one. This file is read by the process systemd starts as you.
#
# THE FOURTH FIELD is a command that must exit 0 for the watcher to be reported healthy. It
# may contain `|`, and unlike a target it IS shell — a target is what systemd starts, so it
# must be a program; an assertion is a question asked once, and the useful ones are pipelines.
# It runs only under `watchd status`, never on a timer, so it costs nothing in the steady
# state. Leave it empty and `status` renders `-`: no assertion was made, which is a different
# fact from one that passed, and a watcher judged on its unit state alone cannot be told from
# a blind one — a process listing, an `is-active` and an unread count are all SILENT for a
# watcher that is reading a database retired underneath it.
#
# WHAT AN ASSERTION SHOULD LOOK FOR IS ITS OWN IDS, NOT FOREIGN ONES. A database here may hold
# beads imported under other prefixes, so "this state names something that is not ours" is
# true of a healthy watcher and would have passed on the blind one. `watchd health-ids
# <file>` asks the question that discriminates: does this state name a single bead of OUR
# prefix. Point it at whatever file the watcher keeps its own memory in.

# PR-NOTIFY has no row: spira-runtime hosts it as the pr-notify handler (spira-runtime/src/watchers).

# VIEW has no row: spira-runtime hosts the cockpit view follower as the view handler (spira-runtime/src/watchers).

# MAIL-DELIVER has no row: the scheduler's mail.arrived source and mail.wake task deliver the wake.

# THE DAEMON ROWS ARE GONE. Every watcher that looped here as a `daemon` row is a task of
# `spira-runtime serve` or retired (scheduler-consolidation-2026-11-03 §1 E, §3 P5), and the
# install that drops a row retires its `spira-watch-<name>` unit. The rows below name logs
# that some other component writes. Hosted, with no row:
#
#   pr-notify, view, inbox-keeper, publish-backlog, round-duty, asks, supersede-duty,
#   brain-dirty, design-asks           spira-runtime handlers (spira-runtime/src/watchers)
#   lc-backup                          task watch.backup_age (lc-backup.timer still runs the backup)
#   round-spill-key                    task watch.spill_key
#   round-ami-stale                    task watch.ami_stale
#   ci-spill-guard                     task watch.ci_spill
#   pass-rate                          task watch.pass_rate
#   warm-full                          task watch.warm_full
#   testenv-cap                        task watch.testenv_cap
#   alarms-probe                       task incident.probe
#   notify (the timer)                 task inbox.notify
#
# Retired (P5): queue-watch (sp-kq4cll), stranded-runner (sp-eb13o2), dwarf-watch (sp-k4y7ax),
# gh-provenance (sp-51hmmm), concierge-guard and its log row (it re-armed the inbox waiter
# that is abolished), and the event-continuity log row (reconcile.continuity raises its
# breaks as inbox lines, so nothing writes event-continuity.log).

# PERF-WATCH is the alarm log of the hot read paths (spira/perf-probes): the watch.perf task
# appends one line per path over the limit, with the top callers lc-serve counted. A quiet log
# means every path answered inside the limit.
perf-watch|log|@SPIRA_RUN@/perf-watch.log

# UNIT-RUN-DBUS learns each user-unit run from the user manager's D-Bus signals and stores one
# unit-run-dbus row per execution in the metrics store. Its log holds only what went wrong (a lost bus
# connection, a refused store), so any line is news.
unit-run-dbus|log|@SPIRA_RUN@/unit-run-dbus.log

# CONCIERGE-INBOX is the durable inbox the Concierge reads. `watchd next concierge-inbox` is how
# it waits: it blocks until a line survives the drop and dedup rules, prints it and exits, with
# the unread count and cursor kept here like any other row.
concierge-inbox|log|@SPIRA_CONCIERGE_INBOX@

# LC-LATENCY is the alarm for a spira-lc method whose p95 over five minutes is past the 500 ms
# ceiling: lc-serve appends one line per breach (re-alarmed every 15 minutes) and nothing else
# writes the log. Brokered tool verbs (groom, queue, ...) and facts-query are batch jobs and exempt.
lc-latency|log|@SPIRA_RUN@/lc-latency.log

# LC-SHIP is the control store's off-box age alarm: lc-serve appends a RED line when the oldest
# staged, not yet uploaded object has waited past SPIRA_LC_SHIP_MAX_AGE_SECS, and a CLEAR line
# when it is back inside it. Nothing else writes the log.
lc-ship|log|@SPIRA_RUN@/lc-ship.log

# MEND-WATCH is the alarm for the mender bounds: the watch.mend task appends one line per
# breach — a MENDING row past its deadline, or a bead mended more than twice in a day — and a
# quiet log means every mender is inside its bounds.
mend-watch|log|@SPIRA_RUN@/mend-watch.log

# SPIRA-SUBMISSION is the SMTP submission listener, now the scheduler's `submission` source, so
# the unit it rides is spira-runtime's. Health TLS-connects to the configured address and
# requires the SMTP banner; unconfigured (SPIRA_SUBMISSION_ADDR empty) it exits 0 and the
# source idles.
spira-submission|extern|runtime|spira-submission health

# RUNTIME-WATCH: the watch.runtime task appends its `cannot check` lines here, so a check that
# cannot run never goes silent.
runtime-watch|log|@SPIRA_RUN@/runtime-watch.log

# SKEW-REFRESH is the alarm for a checkout `skew refresh` keeps declining: the watch.skew task
# appends one line when the same repo has declined three passes running (naming the dirty tracked
# files and any stale index.lock) and again every 180 passes while it persists. A quiet log means
# refreshes are landing.
skew-refresh|log|@SPIRA_RUN@/skew-refresh.log

# ROUNDS-LOOP is where the round supervisor's pages are recorded. Nothing writes the Concierge inbox
# for it directly: it calls `watchd page rounds-loop <text> batch=.. repo=.. members=.. phase=..
# verdict=.. red=..`, which offers the incident to a matching SOP's tool first and puts the page in the
# inbox only when none handled it.
rounds-loop|log|@SPIRA_RUN@/rounds-loop.log
